---

<Base
  title="About — Sarah Barns"
  banner={{
    src: 'https://pub-9840de99aef84197989ef35cf41fa967.r2.dev/docklands/docklands-water.mp4',
    captionTitle: 'Docklands Waterscape',
    captionBody: 'Wurundjuri lands, Naarm/Melbourne.',
  }}
>
  <div class="page-header about-header">
    <h1>About</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="prose practice">
    <p>I'm a scholar-practitioner specialising in <strong>the civic design of digital
      infrastructure</strong>, and <strong>new methods for collaboration</strong> that make this possible. My work mostly spans
      cultural heritage interpretation, digital governance and urban technology.</p>

    <p class="practice-lead">While many urban technologists see the city as data, I see it as an
      archive of noisy events, voices, habitats and governing conditions.</p>

    <p>I deliberately straddle <a href="/research">scholarship</a> and collaborative practice, and lean heavily into live
      experimentation as <a href="/strategy">strategy</a>. I am particularly committed to ensuring the public record is one
      that future generations can continue to be inspired by and shape, with agency and purpose.</p>

    <p>This focus has led to a
      long-standing interest in how certain digital platforms can, by their design, limit access to
      shared digital resources and, over time, produce unhealthy knowledge practices. I wrote a book
      on this called
      <a href="https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-981-32-9725-8"><em>Platform Urbanism</em></a>
      (Palgrave Macmillan, 2019) which examined the historical emergence of global platforms in shaping urban
      policy and infrastructure, and has been cited over 700 times.</p>

    <p>My current research enquiry focuses on the collisions of <strong>AI sovereignties</strong> and <strong>citizen-AI
      activism</strong> in cities, and the governance protocols and pathways that underpin them. My
      disciplinary home is urban digital geography, specifically the historical and political
      geography of digital cities, but much of my practice is also <a href="/creative">creative</a> and collaborative.</p>

    <p>Practice-wise I work with multidisciplinary teams to build and prototype helpful interfaces and story-lines that
      connect us to the places we live in and that sustain us. This work is funded through a range of
      partnerships and collaborations: research fellowships, start-up grants, project commissions,
      and self-funded and government R&amp;D.</p>

    <p>My key platforms are <a href="https://storybox.co">STORYBOX Places</a>,
      <a href="https://civicinterplay.io">Civic Interplay</a> and
      <a href="https://studioesem.com">Studio ESEM</a>, and I maintain a <a href="/listening">listening practice</a> through
      <a href="https://soundcloud.com/soundwalking">Soundwalking</a>.</p>

    <p class="practice-intent">You can read my 2025 Manifesto <a href="/manifesto">here</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose longer">
    <h2 class="about-h2 longer-head">The longer story</h2>

    <p>I came to digital cities out of a concern for what a 'data-driven'
      approach leaves out. I came to archives in recognition that
      technology futures too often like to forget the past. So I try to make a habit of digging,
      retrieving and re-assembling information and collections, and nurturing the possibilities
      that this creates in partnership with others.</p>

    <p>My career began in 2000 at the Department of Communications, Information Technology and the
      Arts, examining the <strong>potential of the information economy</strong> in shaping Australia's knowledge
      economy. This included a focus on <strong>broadband, telecommunications infrastructure</strong>, and the role of
      media policy in shaping Australian public culture. I moved to Sydney to work with former ABC
      chair Mark Armstrong at Network Insight, a think tank dedicated to advancing fit-for-purpose
      media policy in an era of convergence. From there I worked in the ABC's New Media area for a
      spell, before moving to the Australia Council to design cultural and creative industries
      policy, then to the Australian Communications and Media Authority (ACMA), where I commissioned research on the role of the Broadcasting Services Act
      in Australian television and film production, and delivered a national digital media literacy
      research study.</p>

    <p>My PhD (2005&ndash;2010) <strong>used ambient sound archives</strong> to build mobile listening experiences that surfaced
      contested spatial histories, reimagining real-time city technologies as connection points to
      the real times of space. In 2008 that became <em>Sydney Sidetracks</em>, the ABC's first
      location-based service, which let people stand in Martin Place and hear it in 1945. I've
      continued to evolve my <a href="/listening">sound and listening practice</a> for the past
      fifteen years.</p>

    <p>In 2012 I negotiated the release of ABC television and radio archive material <strong>under Creative
      Commons</strong>, through a partnership with ABC Pool called Past Forward. Talbot Duckmanton reporting
      the end of the war, Juanita Nielsen on why she loved the Cross the year before she disappeared,
      Bea Miles, Chow Hayes, Wendy Bacon marking an eviction on Victoria Street.</p>

    <p>In 2013 I led the <strong>ABC's organisation-wide digital strategy</strong>, working across the organisation to
      identify new genres and ways of working that would lead to a refreshed structure for the public
      broadcaster. I built a new <strong>digital emergencies platform</strong> for the ABC to report on emergencies
      using geo-located and social data.</p>

    <p>These experiences in production and collections made me interested in what was happening with
      the emergence of <strong>digital platforms and open data</strong>, and what new technology configurations would
      do to shape future cities. <strong>I called this Platform Urbanism</strong>, and in 2014 was awarded an
      <a href="https://www.urbanstudiesfoundation.org/funding/grantees/dr-sarah-barns/"><strong>international fellowship</strong></a> to pursue this question across technologically advanced global cities.</p>

    <p>So, with a public historian's interest in collections, a digital producer and strategist's
      interest in <strong>digital architecture</strong>, and an urbanist's fascination with the transformation of the
      built environment, I started to build a
      <a href="https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=%22platform+urbanism%22">new field of research</a>.</p>

    <p>I remained interested in how digital methods of installation design could create different
      <strong>experiential interactions with places</strong> as they transform over time, and so with
      Michael Killalea, my creative partner, maintained a creative practice through
      <strong>Studio ESEM</strong>.</p>

    <p>Michael and I founded Esem Projects in 2011. This began
      as a creative idea, and turned into a studio practice running for over a decade. We rebranded as
      <a href="https://studioesem.com">Studio ESEM</a> in 2023, using creative and experiential design
      methods to bring to life the stories that shape places through time. Partners and clients
      include the Australian Museum, the ABC, HASSELL, the NSW Government Architect and many local
      government agencies.</p>

    <p>In 2020 we developed <a href="https://storybox.co">STORYBOX Places</a> as participatory digital
      infrastructure, and have since delivered over forty digital placemaking programs through this
      initiative.</p>

    <p>This practice continues: bringing data, stories, and archives into the open, whether under
      open licences or in shared spaces where they can be encountered in new ways.</p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Strategy and advisory</h2>

    <p>Today I work as a strategic advisor for selected organisations, building on my early career in policy
      and organisational change. I have written <strong>three national strategies</strong> for public agencies: the
      Australia Council's first national creative industries strategy (2004), a national strategy on
      media literacy and communications for the Australian Communications and Media Authority (2008), and the ABC's organisation-wide mobile and
      digital strategy (2013).</p>

    <p>I have since worked on state-based strategy, including the dashboard and data indicators
      strategy for the Greater Sydney Commission and the South Australian Arts Plan, and on data
      strategy for CSIRO and Data61, including for the Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet.
      I have also delivered research and strategy for UCL's Institute for Innovation and Public
      Purpose, Create NSW, the NSW Government Architect and the Australian Museum. Some of my
      strategy projects can be explored <a href="/strategy">here</a>.</p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Civic AI research</h2>

    <p>In 2023 I began to explore the potentials of regenerative data ecosystems with a new research
      fellowship at <a href="https://www.rmit.edu.au/profiles/b/sarah-barns2">RMIT University</a>.
      This enquiry has evolved to address the collision of AI sovereignties with citizen-AI activism
      in cities, and the governance protocols that underpin them. This work is being cultivated
      through a practice I call <a href="https://civicinterplay.io">Civic Interplay</a>, examining
      what new forms of collaboration and digital enquiry are possible in places shaped increasingly
      by agentic, co-intelligent systems.</p>

    <hr>

    <h2>Golden threads</h2>

    <p>Running through the scholarship and studio practice is a
      long-form narrative inquiry into how digital systems reshape place, citizenship and collective
      governance. Golden threads structuring space through time, resisting what Jameson once called 'the actuarial
      colonisation of the unknown'. I am writing a book about it, <em>Still Here</em>, structured as a
      topographical excavation of sites through modes of encounter: listening, reading, installing,
      diagnosing, gathering, letting go.</p>

    <p>I am based in Sydney and Melbourne, Australia. Outside of work I keep a body-based practice through Pilates, yoga,
      cycling and dance, and a long-standing interest in deep-time writing as a counterweight to the
      accelerationist logics of the systems I study.</p>

    <p class="closing-line">Perhaps this can all be put simply: how we draw the lines that shape the
      spines that carry our lives, briefly, on this earth awhile.</p>
  </div>

